A||N||7||FieroJoe||Front bumper mounting stud question..||||5||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||02-22-2001||09:34 PM||||Question about the 8 studs that hold on the front steel bumper, it is what the front energy absorber mounts to.  I had to take it off, because the energy absorber had broken off all of its plastic mounting pieces, so I was going to bolt it back to the steel bumper, so the fascia isnt the only thing holding it up.  While removing the steel bumper, one of the studs snapped, but all others are fine.  Unfortunatly, the stud that broke is one that is right in front of the radiator, so I would have to remove the radiator to put in a new one.<P>Question is, any dangers or risks with going with 7 studs instead of 8??  I can't see off hand why only 1 would make <I>that</I> much difference, but I wanted some more input.<P>Thanks!<P><P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Hank is Here||02-22-2001||09:38 PM||||I think 7 of the stud would be fine.  The purpose of the bar is to get crushed, it is not a laod bearing unit.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||02-22-2001||10:04 PM||||yeah I didnt think so, about it bearing any load.  Just wanted to make sure, thanks alot!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||02-22-2001||11:31 PM||||By your description, I am thinking the stud is the one closest to the center (on the inside of the frame rail).  You don't have to take the radiator out to replace it.  You can reach the hole it goes thru from underneath the car... in front of the radiator.  The outer pair can also be reached from inside the hood (in front of the headlights).  Unfortunately, you may have to replace the stud above/below it too.  They are connected together with a steel strap which keeps them from turning when you tighten the bolts.  Just get yourself some bolts and a lock washer... stick them thru and tighten it up.  I just got done rebuilding the front end of our 87 SC and replaced all the studs on the car with new bolts.<P>If its still too much of a pain to do... I am sure 7 will be sufficient.  BTW, I am assuming the front fascia is off of the car while your working on it. Good luck!  Post back any other ?'s you might have!  I spent a couple weekends playing with it.  <P>------------------<BR>John<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">      <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2m6<BR>1987 Fiero Sport Coupe *In rebuilding*<BR>1997 Ford Probe SE<BR>AOL IM: Fformula88||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||02-23-2001||07:55 AM||||Yes, the front fascia is off.  I have to reattach the energy absorber to the steel bumper.  I guess I didnt try hard enough to get at the back of the bolt, I'll have to try that out.  Thanks for the idea.||||reg||14
Z||000005||deceler8||02-23-2001||09:14 AM||||When I replaced my front fascia, I found several of the push rivets missing, so I reattached the energy absorber with nylon bolts and nuts. I figure nylon will shear if I hit something whereas regular bolts might result in the absorber being torn apart.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||02-23-2001||10:10 AM||||Great idea, I think I will do that, with the nylon hardware.  I'll have to see if we have them big enough at work.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||02-23-2001||04:54 PM||||They sell those nylon push rivets for the energy absorber at Pep Boys... and probably other places.  I do not remember the exact size, but they were the biggest package they had.  Worked great.  You should be able to find those holes from underneath.  Good luck!!||||reg||1
